sen0414:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 1 of the 3rd edition introduces the interactive shell, expressions and their operators with precedence, the integer, floating-point and string data types, string concatenation and replication, variables, and a first program using print, input, len, str, int and float; it closes on how computers store data in binary." ; sen0414:cites sen0414:P01 .
sen0414:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The Python in students' hands has moved since the book: Python 3.14 is the current bugfix release, first released on 2025-10-07, with 3.13 also in bugfix and 3.15's first release scheduled for 2026-10-01. Python 3.13 brought a better interactive interpreter, improved error messages, an experimental free-threaded build and an experimental just-in-time compiler. PEP 701 gave f-strings a formal grammar, making them the natural successor to building strings by concatenation. In the ecosystem, uv - a Python package manager built in Rust - was the most admired technology tag in the 2025 Stack Overflow survey, at 74 per cent." ; sen0414:cites sen0414:P12, sen0414:P07, sen0414:P10, sen0414:P13 .
sen0414:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Where the book stays practical, the reference documentation states what an advanced student should know underneath: Python integers have unlimited precision, while floating-point numbers are usually implemented as C doubles and carry the representation limits the tutorial documents; the Language Reference tabulates operator precedence from most to least binding; and PEP 8 fixes the naming conventions the book's variable examples follow informally." ; sen0414:cites sen0414:P05, sen0414:P04, sen0414:P03, sen0414:P09 .
sen0414:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 1 is best taught as the bridge from the book's basics to the Python students will actually run: the 3.13+ interpreter, f-strings rather than concatenation, precision and representation as real behaviour rather than trivia, and a first look at the ecosystem - uv for packages, and the free-threaded build as the preview of the course's multithreading outcome." ; sen0414:cites sen0414:P07, sen0414:P10, sen0414:P05, sen0414:P04, sen0414:P13 .
sen0414:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Terms used without definition"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter text named tools and ideas that a learner could not look up in the corpus itself: CPython, the interpreter, bytecode, threads, the global interpreter lock, Python Enhancement Proposals, packages and virtual environments; and this record's own inventory listed expression, data type and variable (C01_08, C01_10, C01_12) without the domain ontology carrying them. The Python glossary defines CPython (the canonical implementation, as distributed on python.org), the interpreted language, the bytecode and its virtual machine, the global interpreter lock, free threading and the free-threaded build, the expression and the statement; PEP 1 defines a Python Enhancement Proposal; PEPs 703 and 779 give the three phases by which the lock becomes optional; the threading documentation defines the thread; the tutorial defines the virtual environment; and the uv documentation lists what uv replaces. Each concept section of document 1.1.0 is written from those texts." ; sen0414:cites sen0414:P15, sen0414:P16, sen0414:P17, sen0414:P18, sen0414:P19, sen0414:P20, sen0414:P21, sen0414:P22, sen0414:P23, sen0414:P24, sen0414:P25 .

sen0414:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ;
    sen0414:topicCoverage "27 primary-source concepts inventoried - 7 sections, 6 concepts, 6 built-in functions, 8 operators - all taken from the textbook ontology of the published chapter." ;
    sen0414:citationStrength "25 sources: P01 to P14 opened and verified at 2026-09-24T20:31:40, P15 to P25 at 2026-10-01T17:51:00; 20 cited by findings; the rest (P02, P06, P08, P11, P14) consulted but carrying no claim yet - the deck and page may draw on them." ;
    sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1: primary source identified, concepts enumerated, research conducted, artefact authored; gates Stage1.A-E run below." ;
    sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources; the concept inventory is re-derivable from the textbook ontology." .
